n reduce glycolaldehyde selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Strecker formation of serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated anoxic water; approximately pH 8-9; shaded after photochemical precursor production; sufficient ammonia and cyanide activities."}, "mechanism": "Glycolaldehyde and ammonia establish an imine equilibrium; cyanide addition produces 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) is the supplied evidence for the glycolaldehyde-derived serine precursor. The new stereocenter forms without a chiral influence, so the expected product is racemic. Glycolaldehyde oligomerization, cyanohydrin competition and aminonitrile instability limit selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous general-acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed acidic geothermal water, approximately pH 1-3; protected from UV. Residence time and side-product formation require measurement."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted nitrile hydration and tautomerization generate the corresponding primary amide without changing the C3 skeleton.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) supports serine aminonitrile as a precursor; this node resolves the standard amide intermediate implied by hydrolysis. The supplied review does not establish rapid hydrolysis at neutral ambient conditions. Conditions must balance nitrile conversion against decomposition of the aminohydroxy substrate."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_001"], "ou